.03434,8.69851,8.81341,9.07913,8.69851,8.96944,8.54418,8.19521,8.83272,7.85553,8.52231,8.71249,7.78811,8.07051,8.22781,7.76411,7.77038,8.18431,7.54685,6.87634,7.74737,7.69272,6.97823,7.23734,7.66737]}],"photos":[],"findings":[{"quote":"During the assembly of the MDCA hardware, a conformal coating was applied to the fuel-dispensing needles. The product was applied in two layers, a primer (Dow Corning 1204) and a conformal coat (Dow Corning 3140 RTV (MIL–A–46146) coating). Subsequent testing showed that the coating can both dissolve and “flake” off in both methanol and heptane. When this occurred, the deployed fuel droplet was clearly not pure but contained an unknown amount of the conformal coating.","about":"FLEX fuel needles were coated, and the coating could get into the droplets","note":"All tests in this report (FLEX-001 to FLEX-284).","file":"NTRS-20150023456.pdf","where":"p. 22"},{"quote":"Burning-rate constants were average, and the droplet history was minimally influenced by the contaminant. The contaminant was likely present in trace amounts and was a very low volatility compound. Because methanol and heptane are relatively high volatility fuels, the droplet regression history was dominated by the fuel not by the contaminant.","about":"Contamination: burning-rate constants and droplet histories minimally affected","note":"","file":"NTRS-20150023456.pdf","where":"p. 23"},{"quote":"The MDCA provided two radiometers: a broadband radiom- eter to detect the total radiative output of the flame and a radiometer with a filter to examine the emission from the water vapor. For the tests reported herein, these radiometers were not sensitive enough to provide reliable quantitative information about flame radiometric output, so the data are not reported.","about":"FLEX radiometer data were not sensitive enough to be reliable, so NASA does not report them (version 3)","note":"All tests in this report.","file":"NTRS-20150023456.pdf","where":"p. 10"}]}